course: biology
lesson: 335
title: The garden in autumn: colours, conkers, webs and buried nuts
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Autumn is the garden packing its bags: trees pull useful stuff back out of their leaves, birds stuff their larders or fly south, and the last big jobs of the year get done before the cold.
explain: Kick through a pile of yellow and red leaves and you are walking on a tree's old solar panels. The yellow was hidden under the green all summer; when the tree breaks the green down to save what it can, the yellow shows through, and the reds are freshly made.
explain: On a misty morning every bush is hung with dewy round webs, a jay flaps off with an acorn in its beak to bury, swallows line up on the wires before their long trip to Africa, and a big hairy spider strolls across the living room carpet, making someone scream.
explain: Granny puts conkers in the corners «for the spiders», and the cartoon squirrel remembers every nut it buries. Both stories are fun, and both are wrong, and in this lesson you put them right.
fact: In autumn trees break down the green stuff in their leaves and take back what they can; yellows were hidden under the green all summer, while reds are newly made, and sunny days with cold nights give the brightest reds.
fact: Conkers are the seeds of the horse chestnut; a spiky case usually holds one, sometimes two or three, and conkers are poisonous to eat, unlike sweet chestnuts.
fact: The game of conkers has a world championship held every year in England.
fact: There is no good proof that conkers keep spiders away; a schools' competition tested it and the spiders did not mind the conkers.
fact: Garden spiders build round webs that are big by autumn, dew makes the silk easy to see, and a garden spider can spin a new web in about an hour.
fact: The big spiders seen indoors in autumn are mostly male house spiders wandering in search of a partner; they were already living in the house, hidden, and are harmless to people.
fact: Jays bury thousands of acorns each autumn to eat in winter, a jay is reported to bury up to around 5,000 in one autumn, and the forgotten ones can grow into oak trees.
fact: Swallows gather on wires before flying thousands of kilometres to Africa for the winter, because the flying insects they eat disappear in the cold, and they often return to the same barn.
fact: A fairy ring of mushrooms forms because the fungus spreads out underground and fruits at its edge, so the ring grows wider every year.
fact: Wild mushrooms must never be picked or eaten without an expert, because some are deadly.
fact: Hedgehogs use piles of leaves to build winter nests, so a pile left in a corner helps hedgehogs and insects, and bonfire piles must be checked before they are lit.
fact: An apple cut across shows a star of five seed chambers, each with up to two seeds, so an apple usually holds up to ten seeds.
fact: An apple seed grows into a tree unlike its parent, so every tree of one apple kind is made by joining a twig of it onto the roots of another tree.
exercise: Why do leaves turn yellow in autumn? | The green breaks down and the yellow that was hidden under it shows | The tree pours a yellow juice up from its roots into every leaf before winter | The leaves are painted by frost each night | The sun bleaches them yellow
exercise: Where do the reds of autumn leaves come from? | The tree makes them newly in autumn | They were hidden under the green all summer | They come from the soil | They are made by insects biting the leaves
exercise: What weather gives the brightest red leaves? | Sunny days and cold nights | Grey days and warm, muggy nights | Heavy rain every day for weeks | Snow before the leaves turn
exercise: Why does a tree take back what it can from its leaves before they fall? | To save useful stuff for next spring | To make the leaves lighter to blow away | To feed the hungry worms underneath it | To stop the fallen leaves from rotting
exercise: How many conkers are usually in one spiky case? | One, sometimes two or three | Always exactly four | About twenty | None; the conker grows outside the case
exercise: How are conkers different from sweet chestnuts? | Conkers are poisonous to eat; sweet chestnuts can be roasted | Conkers can be roasted; sweet chestnuts are poisonous | They are the same nut with two names | Conkers grow on the ground, chestnuts on trees
exercise: Do conkers keep spiders out of the house? | There is no good proof; spiders in a test did not mind them | Yes, spiders cannot stand the smell and run away from the room at once | Yes, but only fresh shiny ones | Only if you put one in every room
exercise: Why are spider webs easy to see on a misty autumn morning? | Dew drops cling to the silk | The spiders paint them white | Webs glow faintly in the cold air | Webs are thicker in autumn mornings
exercise: About how long does a garden spider take to spin a new web? | About an hour | About a whole week | About one second | About a whole day and night
exercise: Why do big spiders appear indoors in autumn? | Males wander in search of a partner | They come in from the garden to escape the rain | They are chased in by jays | They come out to eat the conkers
exercise: Are house spiders dangerous to people? | No, house spiders are harmless to people | Only the big ones that come into the house in autumn | Yes, one bite can make you very ill for days | Only if they are bigger than a large coin
exercise: How do jays help to plant forests? | Acorns they bury and forget grow into oaks | They drop seeds from great heights | They carry baby trees in their beaks | They dig holes for squirrels to plant in
exercise: Why can't swallows stay in Britain through the winter? | The flying insects they eat disappear in the cold | Their feathers freeze solid on the first frosty night of winter | Their feathers freeze solid | They are chased out by robins
exercise: Why does a fairy ring of mushrooms get wider every year? | The fungus spreads outward underground and fruits at its edge | Rain washes the mushroom seeds outward a little further every autumn | Fairies dance in a bigger circle each year | Worms push the mushrooms further out
exercise: What should you do before lighting a bonfire? | Check the pile for hedgehogs | Pour milk around it for the hedgehogs | Add a few conkers to scare spiders | Nothing, hedgehogs sleep in trees
exercise: How many seed chambers do you see when you cut an apple across? | Five, in a star | Two, side by side | Ten, in a ring round the edge | One, in the middle
exercise: Why are apple trees of one kind grown by joining twigs onto other roots? | A seed grows into a tree unlike its parent | Roots of apple trees cannot hold water on their own | Apple seeds never grow at all | Twigs grow faster than any root
test: Grandma: «put conkers on the windowsill, love, the spiders can't stand 'em.» How could you test whether that is true? | Watch spiders near conkers and near no conkers in the same kind of place | Put conkers on every windowsill in the house and see if you still find spiders | Ask the spiders | Count the conkers every day
test: «HUGE spider in the bath, kill it, it'll bite me in my sleep» What is true? | It is most likely a harmless male house spider looking for a partner | It came in from the cold garden to hunt sleeping people at night | All big spiders are deadly | It will lay eggs in your ear
test: «bro i'm gonna roast these conkers like at the Christmas market» What is right? | Conkers are poisonous; only sweet chestnuts are roasted | Roasting makes conkers safe, just like the chestnuts at the market | Conkers are sweeter than chestnuts | Only the shell is poisonous
test: In a cartoon a squirrel buries one acorn and digs up exactly that acorn in spring. What do real jays do with the acorns they bury? | They find many again, and forgotten ones can grow into oaks | They never find a single acorn again | They find every acorn without fail | They bury them only to hide them from squirrels
test: What would happen to young oak woods if jays stopped burying acorns? | Fewer new oaks would grow away from the old trees | More oaks would grow than ever | Oaks would start growing conkers | Nothing; jays never touch acorns
test: «mushrooms in a ring on the lawn, fairies were dancing lol. can i eat one» What is right? | It is a fungus spreading underground; never eat wild mushrooms without an expert | Mushrooms that grow in a ring on a lawn are always safe, so you can eat one | It is magic, so it is safe to eat | Only the biggest one is poisonous
test: «just chuck the leaves on the bonfire and light it, who cares» Why should someone check first? | A hedgehog may be sleeping in the pile | Wet leaves make the fire explode | Leaves are against the law to burn | Spiders will come out and bite
test: Count them: 5 swallows sit on the wire and 2 jays perch in the oak. How many tails and how many legs? | 7 tails and 14 legs | 7 tails and 28 legs | 2 tails and 14 legs | 7 tails and 7 legs
test: An apple has five seed chambers with up to two seeds in each. What is the most seeds it might have? | 10 | 5 | 7 | 25
test: «why do swallows sit on wires all bunched up, they're just bored» What are they really doing? | Gathering before flying to Africa for the winter | Drying their feathers in the sun after a long bath in the pond | Waiting for the snow to come | Listening for worms under the road
test: «the cold paints the leaves red, that's what Jack Frost does in the film» What is right? | The tree makes the reds itself; sunny days and cold nights make them brightest | Yes, frost paints every leaf red overnight, and rain then washes the colour off | Leaves turn red from the rain | Only cold nights with no sun make red
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the leaves of many trees assume a yellow colour in autumn. | The green pigment is broken down and withdrawn, revealing a yellow pigment present throughout the summer | A yellow pigment is introduced into the leaves by the action of frost | The leaves absorb a yellow substance from the soil | The yellow colour is produced exclusively by insects feeding upon the leaves
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the efficacy of horse chestnut seeds in repelling spiders. | No reliable evidence supports the claim, and spiders tested showed no aversion | The claim has been conclusively established by numerous investigations | Spiders are repelled only by seeds of a polished appearance | Spiders perish upon contact with such seeds
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the large spiders frequently observed within dwellings in autumn? | They are principally male house spiders, harmless to people, wandering in search of a mate | They are venomous spiders migrating from the garden to avoid frost, and their bite is commonly dangerous | They are juvenile spiders newly hatched within the dwelling | They are spiders driven indoors by the presence of birds
exam: Ascertain the contribution of the jay to the regeneration of oak woodland. | Acorns cached and subsequently not recovered by the bird may germinate as new oaks | Jays consume all acorns before germination can occur, and thereby prevent regeneration | Jays fertilise the soil with acorn shells | Jays prune the branches of mature oaks
